│ semantics under JPA optimistic locking.",                                    │
│     "Compute daily stats by parsing/deriving from clickCount alone without   │
│ persisting timestamps \u2014 rejected: violates FR13/FR5, since aggregate    │
│ counters cannot be attributed to calendar days retroactively.",              │
│     "Introduce a dedicated migration tool (Flyway/Liquibase) to manage the   │
│ new ClickEvent table \u2014 rejected: adds a new dependency requiring        │
│ governance approval; ddl-auto=update on embedded H2 already satisfies schema │
│ evolution needs for this scope.",                                            │
│     "Add the daily endpoint as a new separate controller                     │
│ (DailyStatsController) instead of extending StatsController \u2014           │
│ considered viable but extending StatsController was preferred to keep all    │
│ /api/stats/* handling co-located and minimize new files, reducing review     │
│ surface.",                                                                   │
│     "Perform aggregation in Java after fetching all ClickEvent rows for a    │
│ code instead of via JPQL GROUP BY \u2014 rejected as primary approach:       │
│ pushes unnecessary rows over JDBC and duplicates logic the DB can do         │
│ natively; kept only as an implementation fallback note if H2 date-truncation │
│ functions prove awkward.",                                                   │
│     "Apply the rate limiter to the new GET endpoint for consistency with a   │
│ stricter future policy \u2014 rejected: FR12 explicitly requires no change   │
│ to rate limiter rules/config, and existing GET endpoints are already exempt, │
│ so parity with existing GETs is the correct behavior."                       │
│   ],                                                                         │
│   "risks": [                                                                 │
│     {                                                                        │
│       "risk": "H2's DATE()/CAST(... AS DATE) truncation on a stored          │
│ Instant/timestamp column may use JVM/session default timezone instead of     │
│ true UTC, causing calendar-day misattribution near midnight.",               │
│       "impact": "Clicks near day boundaries could be grouped into the wrong  │
│ date, violating FR5/FR6, and could differ between environments with          │
│ different default timezones.",                                               │
│       "mitigation": "Store clickedAt as an Instant mapped to a TIMESTAMP     │
│ column with explicit UTC semantics, and set                                  │
│ spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.time_zone=UTC (and/or JVM               │
│ -Duser.timezone=UTC) so the DB session normalizes to UTC; alternatively      │
│ compute the day-truncation in Java                                           │
